 And, um, yeah, they tell you right away, uh, that don't bring anything in there that

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:22.680
 you're like loose, that you're, that you are not okay with losing, um, like jewelry and

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:27.300
 whatnot, because if it does sink to the bottom, no one's, no one's going down there to get

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:27.460
 it.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:33.980
 Um, but I guess it's like a common practice, uh, or it was a common practice to, yeah, throw

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:36.740
 like sacrifices in there.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:42.420
 And, and, and, and, you know, sometimes they were literal, uh, and sometimes they were metaphorical,

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:45.000
 I think, but, so there's definitely like treasure down there.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:47.580
 And like, I guess they valued silver a lot.

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:51.440
 That was like, they, they, gold, not so much is my understanding.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:58.380
 Like their word for gold was kind of like, um, you know, like, uh, it's like a, like a

00:12:58.380 --> 00:12:59.880
 sun turd, I guess.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:04.580
 I don't know a more graceful way of saying that, but that was basically what they called

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:04.740
 it.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:10.240
 And they didn't value it as, as much as, as they valued the silver, I guess.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:17.980
 Um, and so anyways, we went to these cenotes after, um, and then we'd like, they would take

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:19.240
 us out to lunch and whatnot.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:20.900
 And that was kind of the excursion.

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:23.060
 So we did three of them.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:25.580
 First was, uh, Chichen Itza.

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:34.160
 The second was a place called, uh, Coba, which is less well, I would say of the three that

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:36.900
 we went to, it was definitely the least touristy.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:39.880
 And it was definitely my, my favorite.

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:48.540
 Um, because when you get there, you can't like drive up to it or even close to it.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:54.240
 Like the parking lot is at least three miles away from the pyramid.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:14:02.420
 So it's very common for people to rent, um, bicycles and, you know, just like a couple

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:07.660
 bucks, um, get a bike, uh, and, and, and ride out there.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:12.540
 And, and as you're riding along, you can kind of see other structures, which is cool.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:20.800
 Um, and like there are buildings that they, they say were like observatories, uh, and things

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:21.320
 like that.

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:25.780
 And, um, yeah, this one is very cool because it's big.

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:33.440
 So the one in, uh, Coba was called Nohawk Mool, I think, and I have no idea if I pronounced

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:35.840
 that right, but something like that.

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:38.980
 And it's, it's the tallest one in that area.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:43.580
 It's not the tallest one overall, but it's, it's the tallest one in that, uh, Yucatan

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:44.020
 area.

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:50.400
 And, and what's cool about it is it, it is, it's one of the only ones left that they'll

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:51.020
 let you climb.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:59.280
 And they had shut it down for a couple of years because people, and so in order to get

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:04.220
 up to the top, like, you know, the pyramids, like, uh, like there are steps, right.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:09.100
 But it's not like a step, uh, like you would walk up the stairs, like these, they're pretty

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:10.380
 steep steps.

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:15.420
 And, um, coming down is, is definitely nerve wracking.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:21.640
 Uh, so people would, would kind of like slide on their butt and, and go down one step at

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:22.000
 a time.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:26.480
 But I think that at some point they realized that, that that was kind of a recipe for disaster

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:29.700
 and they shut it down for a couple of years and they put in some stairs.

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:37.300
 And so now there are like actual stairs to help you navigate up and it's still nerve wracking.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:45.100
 Um, but yeah, it's, it's cool and you can climb up there and, and basically they, what, what

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:49.180
 they told us was that the, the height of the pyramid is sort of arbitrary.

